    <page>
        <exercise>
            <introduction>
                <p>
                    During a winter snowstorm, the town of Autumndale deploys
                    its fleet of snowplows after 4 inches of snow have
                    accumulated on the streets.
                    Assume that the snow continues to fall at a rate of
                    <m>\frac{3}{5}</m> inches per hour, and that the snowplows
                    can remove the snow at a rate that is equal to 0.45 times
                    the amount of snow on the ground per hour.
                    Using this information, complete the following.
                </p>
            </introduction>

            <task workspace="1in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Formulate an initial value problem that can be used to
                        model the depth of the snow on the ground in Autumndale.
                    </p>
                </statement>

                <solution component="10-solutions">
                    <p>
                        <m>\frac{dS}{dt}=\frac{3}{5}−0.45s</m> Here, <m>S</m> is
                        the amount of snow at time <m>t</m>, where <m>S</m> is
                        the dependent variable and <m>t</m> is the independent
                        variable.
                    </p>
                </solution>
            </task>

            <task workspace="1in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Find an analytic solution to the initial value problem.
                    </p>
                </statement>

                <solution component="10-solutions">
                    <p>
                        We can find the analytic solution by using the method of
                        separation of variables.
                        <md>
                            \begin{array}{c}
                        \frac{dS}{dt} \amp = \amp \frac{3}{5} - 0.45S \\
                        \frac{dS}{dt} \amp = -0.45\left(-\frac{4}{3} + S\right) \\
                        \int \frac{1}{-\frac{4}{3} + S}dS \amp = \amp \int -0.45dt \\
                        \ln(-\frac{4}{3} + S) \amp = \amp -0.45t + C \\
                        S(t) \amp = \amp \frac{4}{3} + Ce^{-0.45t}.
                        \end{array}
                        </md>
                        Then, use the initial condition that at time <m>t=0</m>
                        the plows start with <m>S = 4</m>.
                        <md>
                            \begin{array}{c}
                        S(0) \amp = \amp 4 = \frac{4}{3} + Ce^{0} \\
                        C \amp = \amp 4 - \frac{4}{3} \\
                        C \amp = \amp \frac{8}{3} \\
                        \end{array}
                        </md>
                        This yields an analytic solution of
                        <m>{S(t) = \frac{4}{3} + \frac{8}{3}e^{-0.45t}}</m>.
                    </p>
                </solution>
            </task>

            <task workspace="1.5in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Find any fixed points and determine their stability.
                    </p>
                </statement>

                <solution component="10-solutions">
                    <p>
                        Fixed points occur when the first derivative is equal to
                        zero.
                        Therefore,
                        <md>
                            \frac{dSt}{dt} = 0 \Rightarrow \frac{3}{5} - 0.45S = 0 \Rightarrow S = \frac{4}{3}.
                        </md>
                        To test the stability, we can consider the derivative of
                        the differential equation with respect to the dependent
                        variable.
                        The derivative of <m>\frac{dS}{dt}</m> is <m>-0.45</m>.
                        This is less than zero, and thus the fixed point is
                        stable.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        <em>
                            Note that in general, to test stability, a phase portrait
                            works.  However, you can also look at the derivative of the 
                            differential equation with respect to the dependent 
                            variable.  If this derivative evaluated at the fixed point 
                            is negative, then the fixed point is stable. If it is positive,
                            this means that the fixed point is unstable.</em>
                    </p>
                </solution>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Plot the solution that represents the depth of the snow
                        on the ground for an appropriate amount of time.
                        In addition, plot the solution using Python's internal
                        ODE solver.
                    </p>
                </statement>

                <solution component="10-solutions">
                    <program language="python">
                        <code>
import math
import numpy as np
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt
from scipy.integrate import odeint

S0 = 4

def analytic_model(t):
  S = 1.33 + 2.67*np.exp(-0.45*t)
  return S

def num_model(S,t):
  dSdt = 3/5 - 0.45*S
  return dSdt

t =np.linspace(0,50,5000)

y1 = odeint(num_model,S0,t)
y2 = analytic_model(t)
plt.plot(t,y1,label = 'Numerical Snow Depth')
plt.plot(t,y2,label = 'Analytic Snow Depth')
plt.xlabel('time')
plt.ylabel('')
plt.legend()
plt.show()
                        </code>
                    </program>

                    <image source="images/10-SnowDepth.png" width="75%">
                        <shortdescription>
                            Graph of the snow depth for the differential
                            equation model representing plowing the snow.
                            This graphs the analytic and numerical solutions.
                        </shortdescription>
                    </image>
                </solution>
            </task>

            <task workspace="1in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        How do the different graphs above compare?
                        How <q>bad</q> is the error?
                    </p>
                </statement>

                <solution component="10-solutions">
                    <p>
                        The graphs are nearly identical.
                        We can look at the error numerically.
                        The error is very small.
                        Note that if you truncate the coefficients and constants
                        when solving the differential equation, the error will
                        be larger.
                    </p>

                    <program language="python">
                        <code>
error = np.abs(y2 - y1[:,0])
plt.plot(t,error)
plt.xlabel('time')
plt.ylabel('Error')
plt.show()
                        </code>
                    </program>

                    <image source="images/10-SnowPlowError.png" width="75%">
                        <shortdescription>
                            Error between the analytic and numerical solutions
                            for the Snow Depth.
                        </shortdescription>
                    </image>
                </solution>
            </task>

